# TypeScript conventions

## Options object for 3 or more parameters

When a function has **3 or more parameters**, take a single options object with a declared interface. TypeScript has no named arguments; this is the equivalent guarantee that call sites are readable.

```ts
// 2 params — positional is fine
resolver.match(spoken, limit);

// 3 or more — options object required
interface MatchOptions {
  spoken: string;
  limit: number;
  contextSet?: SetCode;
  minScore?: number;
}

resolver.match({
  spoken: "lightning bolt",
  limit: 5,
  contextSet: SetCode.of("BLB"),
  minScore: 0.7,
});
```

If an edit pushes a function from fewer than 3 parameters to 3 or more, convert it and update all call sites in the same change.

## Types

- No `any`. Use `unknown` at boundaries and parse into a known type.
- Parse external input (HTTP bodies, WebSocket frames, CSV rows, third-party JSON) with Zod. Never cast.
- No non-null assertions (`!`). Handle the absent case.
- Explicit return types on exported functions.
- Prefer `type` for unions and `interface` for object contracts and ports.

## Errors

Never swallow an error.

```ts
// BAD
try { await sync(); } catch {}

// GOOD
try {
  await sync();
} catch (error) {
  logger.error("Archidekt sync failed", { error });
  throw new SyncFailedError("Could not push collection", { cause: error });
}
```
